To configure your Chrome extension's boilerplate files to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage, you need to create a content script file and add it to your extension's manifest file. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Create a new file named `content_script.js` in your extension's folder.
2. Open `content_script.js` and add the following code:
```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  const script = document.createElement('script');
  script.src = 'path/to/your/javascript/file.js';
  document.body.appendChild(script);
});
```
Replace `'path/to/your/javascript/file.js'` with the actual path to your JavaScript file.
3. Add `"content_scripts": [{"matches": ["<all_urls>"], "js": ["content_script.js"]}]` to your extension's manifest file (manifest.json). This will tell Chrome to inject the `content_script.js` file into all webpages the user visits.
4. Update your extension's manifest file (manifest.json) to include the necessary permissions for your content script to run. For example, if your JavaScript file accesses the user's data, you'll need to add the `"permissions": ["activeTab", "tabs", "https://*/*", "http://*/*"]` property to your manifest file.
5. Save both files and reload your Chrome browser to apply the changes.

Now, when you visit a webpage, your Chrome extension will inject the specified JavaScript file into the page.

