Here's a simple React utility that removes widows from a text. Widows are words or phrases that appear at the end of a line, making the line look unbalanced. This utility uses the `wordWrap` CSS property to wrap words within a container and ensures that no word ends up at the end of a line.

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const [wrappedText, setWrappedText] = useState('');

  const handleWrapText = () => {
    const containerWidth = 300; // Adjust the width as needed
    const lines = text.split('\n');
    const wrappedLines = lines.map(line => {
      const words = line.split(' ');
      const lastIndex = words.length - 1;
      const lastWord = words[lastIndex];

      if (lastWord.length > containerWidth) {
        words.splice(lastIndex, 1);
        return words.join(' ') + '\n';
      }

      return line;
    });

    setWrappedText(wrappedLines.join('\n'));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={handleWrapText}>Remove Widows</button>
      <pre style={{ whiteSpace: 'pre-wrap', wordWrap: 'break-word' }}>{wrappedText}</pre>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this utility, simply pass in the text you want to remove widows from, like this:

```jsx
<RemoveWidows text="Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Sed non risus. Suspendisse lectus tortor, dignissim sed, adipiscing nec, ultricies sed, dolor. Cras mattis consectetur purus, sed tincidunt magna aliquet non. Donec nec justo eget felis facilisis malesuada. Aliquam erat volutpat." />
```

This will display the text with removed widows,

To update your application without replacing the existing EXE, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a new directory on the user's machine to store the updated files.
2. Extract the contents of the ZIP file into the new directory.
3. Update the configuration files (e.g., app.config, web.config) with the new settings.
4. Replace the old DLLs and other supporting files with the new ones.
5. Start the application using the updated files.

Here's some sample code to demonstrate how you can implement this approach:
```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Linq;
using System.Reflection;
using System.Windows.Forms;

namespace UpdateApplication
{
    static class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            string applicationPath = Path.GetDirectoryName(Assembly.GetEntryAssembly().Location);
            string updateZipFile = Path.Combine(applicationPath, "update.zip");
            string updateDirectory = Path.Combine(applicationPath, "update");

            if (!Directory.Exists(updateDirectory))
            {
                Directory.CreateDirectory(updateDirectory);
            }

            File.Copy(updateZipFile, Path.Combine(updateDirectory, "update.zip"), true);

            string configFile = Path.Combine(applicationPath, "app.config");
            string updatedConfigFile = Path.Combine(updateDirectory, "app.config");

            if (File.Exists(updatedConfigFile))
            {
                File.Copy(updatedConfigFile, configFile, true);
            }

            string dllFile = Path.Combine(applicationPath, "MyLibrary.dll");
            string updatedDllFile = Path.Combine(updateDirectory, "MyLibrary.dll");

            if (File.Exists(updatedDllFile))
            {
                File.Copy(updatedDllFile, dllFile, true);
            }

            // Replace other supporting files as needed

            Process.Start(Path.Combine(updateDirectory, "my_application.exe"));
        }
    }
}
```
